quick_facts: Zero charge · mass 0.14% above the proton's
quick_facts: James Chadwick discovered it (1932)
quick_facts: Free neutrons decay in ~15 minutes
quick_facts: Only slow ('thermal') neutrons efficiently split U-235
quick_facts: Neutrons damage crystal lattices — the 'Wigner effect'

sources:
    {"organization":"Britannica","title":"Neutron","url":"https://www.britannica.com/science/neutron"}

definition:
    The neutral particle sharing the atomic nucleus with protons — almost exactly as heavy (1.67×10⁻²⁷ kg), just 0.14% heavier, and carrying no electric charge. That neutrality is its superpower: it slips through electron clouds and into nuclei without electrical resistance, which makes it both the trigger of fission chains and a deep-penetrating probe of matter. Freed from a nucleus a neutron survives about 15 minutes before decaying into a proton, an electron, and an antineutrino.

debunking_snippet:
    “Neutrons are dangerous because they're charged.” The opposite — their danger comes from having no charge at all: nothing repels them, so they penetrate deeply and can even turn stable atoms radioactive.
